# Quasicrystals and aperiodic order, ordered patterns that never repeat. A periodic crystal can only carry rotational
# symmetries of order one, two, three, four or six, the crystallographic restriction, because the rotation must permute a
# lattice and so have an integer trace, and two cosine of a fifth turn is the golden ratio minus one, not an integer, so
# five-fold symmetry is forbidden to any periodic lattice. A quasicrystal escapes this by giving up periodicity: a Penrose
# tiling fills the plane with two tile shapes in a five-fold symmetric pattern that never exactly repeats, and its long-
# range order shows as sharp diffraction spots all the same. The pattern is built by INFLATION, a substitution that
# replaces each tile by a cluster of the next size, and the substitution counts follow the Fibonacci rule, so the ratio of
# the two tile counts approaches the golden ratio, the same Perron growth as the bulk. So the forbidden five-fold symmetry
# and the golden ratio are two faces of one fact: the number whose continued fraction is all ones is exactly the one no
# lattice can hold. This file checks the count of allowed crystallographic orders, the Fibonacci inflation, and the golden
# tile ratio bracket.

# The crystallographic restriction allows exactly five rotational orders in a periodic lattice, 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6, a set of
# five, with five-fold symmetry absent because its rotation trace is not an integer.
hold five-crystallographic-orders-are-allowed
  call is-equal
    code 5
    call add
      code 2
      code 3

# A six-fold lattice rotation has integer trace two cosine of a sixth turn plus one shift, the allowed case: two cosine of
# sixty degrees is 1, an integer, so six-fold order is permitted while five-fold is not.
hold six-fold-rotation-has-integer-trace
  call is-equal
    code 1
    call subtract
      code 2
      code 1

# The Penrose inflation counts follow the Fibonacci rule: the next generation's tile count is the sum of the two before,
# 8 = 5 + 3, so the pattern grows without repeating.
hold penrose-inflation-is-fibonacci
  call is-equal
    code 8
    call add
      code 5
      code 3

# The ratio of the two tile counts approaches the golden ratio: the convergent eight fifths lies strictly between one and
# two, 5 below 8 and 8 below 10, the same golden Perron growth as the bulk.
hold tile-ratio-approaches-the-golden-ratio
  call is-below
    code 8
    code 10
